\part{Language-building tools}
\chapter{Composition, clarity, and a readable page}\label{ch:specimen}
\begin{MuObjectives}
\item Recognize the hierarchy of the toolkit and its implementation contracts.
\item Read a listing without losing the thread of the surrounding explanation.
\end{MuObjectives}
MuDSL packages language-building blocks as orthogonal features. Compose the
features you need, describe your domain, and let the host language perform the
work. A book about that process should be just as composable: its typography
should reveal structure rather than compete with it.

The entry points are \api{MuDSL.hpp} and \api{MuDSL.d}. A literal name such as
\api{mull::Runtime::mullier.lastIR.dump()} can wrap in a narrow measure, as can
\api{share/mull/stdlib/collections/sequence.mull}. Special characters remain
literal: \api{a_b<T>&value}, \api{$E?}, and \api{#include}.
\footnote{Footnotes use a hanging marker and stay below the main reading area.}
\section{A small, complete example}\label{sec:composition}
The same design serves short fragments and full source appendices. Listings
remain selectable text, keep their indentation, and can break across pages.
\begin{cppcode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
struct Language : dsl::DSL<Language, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};
auto value = Language::wrap(5)
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; });
// A deliberately long line checks the continuation marker and the available measure without changing the source text or shrinking every listing to an unreadable size.
\end{cppcode}
\begin{mullcode}
val values <- [1, 2, 3];
return values;
\end{mullcode}
\begin{dcode}
import MuDSL;
struct Language { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, Operators); }
\end{dcode}
\begin{rulecode}
(binary "and" (literal "true") (number "42"))
\end{rulecode}
\begin{code}
make -C mudsl/book check
\end{code}
\subsection{Math belongs to the same book}
\subsubsection{A shared mathematical vocabulary}
The body and mathematics share a family. A simple identity is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sum}
  \sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \frac{n(n+1)}{2},\qquad n\in\mathbb{N}.
\end{equation}
\begin{MuDefinition}{composition}
The operation that connects the output of one transformation to the input of
another, while preserving the contract between them.
\end{MuDefinition}
\begin{MuExample}{Follow the data}\label{ex:data}
The pipeline in \cref{sec:composition} produces eight. Compare its explicit
control flow with the notation in \cref{eq:sum}.
\end{MuExample}
